      "writtenOn": "2024-03-21T20:14:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This looks like a very heavy way to specify an interface like\n\n```\nclass Callbacks {\n    std::unique_ptr\u003cSkShaper\u003e makeShaper(sk_sp\u003cSkFontMgr\u003e fallback)\u003e \u003d 0;\n    std::unique_ptr\u003cSkShaper::BiDiRunIterator\u003e makeBiDiIterator(\n        const char* utf8, size_t utf8Bytes, uint8_t bidiLevel) \u003d 0;\n    std::unique_ptr\u003cSkShaper::ScriptRunIterator\u003e makeScriptIterator(\n        const char* utf8, size_t utf8Bytes)\u003e \u003d 0;\n};\n````\n\nAlso, just passing this around by value would be just as heavy as (or less heavy than) passing around the tuple by value, but it seems if these Callbacks have state it might be better to move them or reference them somehow? Also, this class is kind of the definition of a Factory, which is a special kind of callback, so that might be a better name.",
